<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Abbey of Fulda</h1><p class="st-meta">1220 CE – 1802 CE (approx.) · capital Fulda</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Abbey founded by a disciple of Saint Boniface, a centre of Carolingian learning, and later a baroque prince-bishopric in eastern Hesse.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sturmi">Sturm</a>, a disciple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a>, founded the monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a> in 744, and Boniface was buried there in 754. Under Abbot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hrabanus-maurus">Hrabanus Maurus</a> in the 9th century it became one of the great centres of learning in the Frankish realm. Its abbots gathered vast estates and were recognised as imperial princes by 1220.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The prince-abbots ruled a sizeable territory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhon">Rhön</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a> valley, with the support of a powerful noble chapter. Abbot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balthasar-von-dernbach">Balthasar von Dernbach</a> led a fierce <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> in the late 16th century. The baroque cathedral by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-dientzenhofer">Johann Dientzenhofer</a> and the abbots' palace reshaped the town, and in 1752 the abbey was raised to a prince-bishopric.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Secularised in 1802, Fulda went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-of-the-netherlands">William Frederick of Nassau-Orange</a>, then to French control and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-duchy-of-frankfurt">Grand Duchy of Frankfurt</a>. In 1816 it passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-hesse">Electorate of Hesse</a>. The diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a> continues today.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-abbey-of-fulda.jpg" alt="Prince-Abbey of Fulda" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Laurens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_St_George%27s_Cross.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1220 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Fulda</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sturm, Hrabanus Maurus, Balthasar von Dernbach</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Prince-Bishopric of Fulda, Hochstift Fulda</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1511&amp;focus=prince-abbey-of-fulda" aria-label="Prince-Abbey of Fulda on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4901 422 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Abbey of Fulda lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5221 678L5229 694M5221 678L5183 683"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5183 683h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5221 678h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5221 678h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Fulda. <a href="/atlas?year=1511&amp;focus=prince-abbey-of-fulda">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
